      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Collaboration with Francis Crick</strong><br>\nWatson and Crick's collaboration was intense and fruitful, with both scientists bringing their unique perspectives to the table. Crick's expertise in X-ray diffraction and Watson's background in genetics were the perfect combination for their DNA model. Their work was based on the X-ray diffraction images produced by Rosalind Franklin, although she was not initially credited for her critical contribution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Impact of the Double Helix</strong><br>\nThe discovery of the double helix structure of DNA revolutionized the way scientists understood heredity and genetic information. It paved the way for numerous advancements in biology and medicine, including the Human Genome Project. Watson and Crick's model showed how DNA replicates and how genetic information is coded within it.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize and Recognition</strong><br>\nIn 1962, Watson, Crick, and Maurice Wilkins were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for their discovery of the molecular structure of DNA. The award recognized their contribution to science and highlighted the importance of understanding DNA for all life sciences.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
