          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-Nobel Career</strong><br>\nFollowing his Nobel win, Watson continued to be a prominent figure in the scientific community. He worked at Harvard University, where he focused on teaching and research. In 1968, he published \"The Double Helix,\" a personal account of the discovery of DNA's structure, which became a best-selling book.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Science Administration</strong><br>\nJames Watson's influence extends beyond his research. He became involved in science administration and advocacy. He helped to launch the Human Genome Project and was appointed as the project's first director, which aimed to map all human genes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Views</strong><br>\nWatson is known for his outspoken nature and has been a public figure in debates on genetic research. In his later years, he has faced criticism for controversial comments on topics such as intelligence and race. Despite the controversies, his contributions to genetics and the study of DNA remain invaluable.</p>\n"
              },
